import {section,table,cite} from './topic-tools.js';
const scrollTable=(caption,heads,rows)=>table(caption,heads,rows).replace('<div class="dense-table-wrap">','<div class="dense-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable GLM release comparison">');
export function glmFlashEvidence(){return section('glm-flash-evidence','GLM-5.3-Flash: inspect the whole comparison',`
<p>Reduced active computation is useful only if the resulting model preserves the capabilities the workload needs. Start with the base checkpoint: it precedes the agent interface and post-training changes that can dominate deployed comparisons.</p>
${scrollTable('Release base-checkpoint comparison; reported benchmark scores',['Benchmark','GLM-5-Base','GLM-5.3-Flash-Base'],[
['MMLU','88.3','88.1'],['BBH','87.4','86.6'],['HellaSwag','88.1','87.1'],['LiveCodeBench-Base','34.4','37.6'],['SimpleQA','36.0','33.5']])}
<p>Flash improves the reported coding-base score, but scores lower on all four listed general or factual benchmarks. This is a capability tradeoff, not uniform domination. It also does not isolate the hybrid attention design: data, scale and optimization can differ between these runs.</p>
<p>Post-trained comparisons answer a different question: how well does the complete model, tools and harness solve the task? The comparator columns expose workload differences that disappear if we select only each model's best result.</p>
${scrollTable('Release post-trained systems; benchmark versions are part of the measurement',['Benchmark','GLM-5.3-Flash','GLM-5.2','Claude Opus 4.8'],[
['Terminal-Bench 2.1','84.3','81.0','85.0'],['DeepSWE v1.1','63.4','46.2','58.0'],['NL2Repo','56.3','48.9','69.7'],['Toolathlon Verified','78.4','59.9','76.2'],['AutomationBench v1.0.6','48.8','26.2','41.0'],["Agents' Last Exam",'26.3','20.4','27.0'],['HLE with tools','55.3','54.7','57.9']])}
<p>For example, Flash exceeds the listed GLM-5.2 scores throughout this subset. Against Claude, the directions differ: DeepSWE, Toolathlon and AutomationBench favor Flash, while Terminal-Bench, NL2Repo, Agents' Last Exam and HLE favor Claude. A single mean would hide those workload differences.</p>
<p>The release uses Claude Code for Terminal-Bench and mini-swe-agent for DeepSWE. NL2Repo uses a 1M context; HLE includes tools and context management. Other differences include sampling, context limits, model judges and tool access. These conditions prevent an architecture-only reading of the table.</p>
<p>The model card specifies maximum reasoning effort for reproduction. Terminal-Bench uses Claude Code 2.1.207, temperature 1, top-p 1, 65,536 maximum new tokens and a six-hour timeout. DeepSWE uses temperature 0.95, top-p 1, 400K context and a six-hour timeout. NL2Repo uses temperature 1, top-p 1 and 64K maximum new tokens. HLE uses temperature 1, top-p 0.95, 163,840 maximum generated tokens, 300K context and GPT-5.6-luna (medium) judging. Toolathlon reports pass@1 averaged across three independent runs. AutomationBench v1.0.6 includes the null-type handling fix. These are release-specific contracts, not recommended settings for every task.</p>
<h3>Visual scores need their own task contract</h3>
<p>For multimodal tasks, the release reports OfficeQA Pro 62.4, CharXiv Reasoning with tools 89.4, Chartography with tools 78.0 and MVBench 77.8. These are four different evaluations, not four estimates of one general visual ability. Tool-assisted reasoning is not directly interchangeable with a no-tool perception score.</p>
<p>Visual coding adds a render–inspect–revise loop: code produces an artifact, the agent inspects that artifact, and environment feedback guides another edit. User-flow verification can test layout, interaction behavior, GUI judgment and task success beyond compilation. The release does not disclose dataset size, environment count, rollout volume, reward formula, verifier accuracy or a controlled ablation for this loop.</p>
<h3>Keep operational claims separate from quality</h3>
<p>The provider's 3× end-to-end serving improvement compares with its initial implementation on the same hardware. Its 3.0× attention-compute and 4.4× BF16 KV-cache comparisons against GLM-5.3 use architectural accounting, without a complete reproducible formula table. Neither is a universal cross-model latency guarantee.</p>
<p>A “one-tenth the price” product claim is also a snapshot. Utilization, batching, cache hits, quantization, hardware and subsidies can change price without changing the checkpoint. Low active parameters do not remove 320B-scale storage or expert communication costs.</p>
<p>The 30T multimodal training claim lacks a disclosed modality mixture, deduplication recipe, optimizer, schedule and compute budget. Released weights and code can establish implementation structure, but cannot supply these missing run details. ${cite('https://z.ai/blog/glm-5.3-flash','Z.ai release')} · ${cite('https://huggingface.co/zai-org/GLM-5.3-Flash','Model card and weights')}</p>
`);}
